/* Lesson quizzes — batch 9 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p21m1":[
 {id:"p21m1q1", q:"A company runs 1,200 Windows workstations with Sysmon, each producing about 50 MB of logs a day. Ignoring servers and network devices, roughly what is the daily SIEM ingest, and how much searchable history does 90 days of retention require?", options:["6 GB a day; about 540 GB for 90 days","60 GB a day; about 5.4 TB for 90 days","60 GB a day; about 22 TB for 90 days","600 GB a day; about 54 TB for 90 days"], answer:1, why:"1,200 × 50 MB = 60,000 MB, about 60 GB a day, and 60 GB × 90 days is about 5.4 TB. The 22 TB figure is a full year (60 × 365), not the 90 days asked; the other two options slip a decimal place."},
 {id:"p21m1q2", q:"A detection rule fires 120 times a day. Investigation shows 6 of those alerts are real, and each alert takes 4 minutes to triage. What are the rule's precision and its daily triage cost?", options:["95% precision; about 8 analyst-hours a day","50% precision; about 8 analyst-hours a day","5% precision; about 24 analyst-minutes a day","5% precision; about 8 analyst-hours a day"], answer:3, why:"Precision is true positives ÷ all alerts = 6 ÷ 120 = 5%. Every alert must be triaged, not just the real ones, so the cost is 120 × 4 = 480 minutes, or 8 hours. Counting only the 6 true positives gives the misleading 24 minutes."},
 {id:"p21m1q3", q:"Your SIEM has 40 low-fidelity rules (new scheduled task, rare parent process, sign-in from a new country). Each is noisy alone, but real intrusions usually trip several of them on the same host or user. Which design keeps their value while cutting pages?", options:["Score each rule's hits per host or user, and page only when the total crosses a threshold","Delete the rules, because a rule with low precision on its own adds no value to the SOC","Raise all 40 rules to high severity so that Tier 1 analysts give each alert closer attention","Keep paging on every alert, but require Tier 1 to close each one within sixty seconds"], answer:0, why:"Risk-based alerting lets weak signals accumulate per host or user, so only the combination that real intrusions produce pages someone. Deleting the rules throws that signal away, and raising severity or rushing triage feeds alert fatigue instead of curing it."},
 {id:"p21m1q4", q:"During triage you find that, minutes after a malicious attachment ran, a scheduled task was created that launches a hidden script at every logon. Where does this step sit in the Cyber Kill Chain and in ATT&CK?", options:["Delivery; ATT&CK tactic Initial Access","Command and control; ATT&CK tactic Command and Control","Installation; ATT&CK tactic Persistence","Actions on objectives; ATT&CK tactic Exfiltration"], answer:2, why:"A scheduled task that survives reboots is the attacker installing a foothold: the Kill Chain calls it installation and ATT&CK files it under Persistence (T1053.005). Delivery and Initial Access describe how the attachment arrived, which happened earlier."},
 {id:"p21m1q5", q:"Threat intelligence on a group targeting your sector says it rotates domains daily and recompiles its loader for each victim, but always has an Office document launch PowerShell with an encoded command. Which detection will stay useful the longest?", options:["A blocklist of the file hashes listed in the report","An alert on Office apps spawning PowerShell with an encoded command","A DNS blocklist of the domains named in the report","A firewall rule blocking the report's command-and-control IP addresses"], answer:1, why:"Office spawning encoded PowerShell is a TTP at the top of the Pyramid of Pain; the group must change how it operates to evade it. Hashes, domains and IP addresses sit at the bottom and, by the report's own description, change with every victim."}
],
"p21m2":[
 {id:"p21m2q1", q:"A threat hunt searches 90 days of SIEM data for PowerShell/Operational Event 4104 and finds zero events across 800 Windows hosts, even though administrators use PowerShell every day. What is the most likely explanation?", options:["No malicious PowerShell ran anywhere in the environment during those 90 days","An attacker cleared the PowerShell log on all 800 hosts to hide what they ran","Script block logging is not enabled, or that log channel is not forwarded to the SIEM","Event 4104 records failed logons, not PowerShell, so the hunt queried the wrong event entirely"], answer:2, why:"Administrators use PowerShell daily, so a working 4104 source could not be empty. Absence of a log is usually absence of logging: script-block logging must be enabled by policy and the channel collected. Concluding that nothing malicious ran trusts a source that is not there."},
 {id:"p21m2q2", q:"These Sysmon Event 1 records come from a web server and two workstations. Which parent-child pair should be escalated on sight?", options:["ParentImage w3wp.exe creating powershell.exe","ParentImage explorer.exe creating chrome.exe","ParentImage services.exe creating svchost.exe","ParentImage explorer.exe creating notepad.exe"], answer:0, why:"An IIS worker process serves web requests and has no legitimate reason to start a shell, so w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell is the classic web-shell signature. The other pairs are how Windows normally starts browsers, services and user programs."},
 {id:"p21m2q3", q:"You must list every internal host that talked to a known C2 address at any point in the last six months. Retention: proxy logs 30 days, full packet capture 3 days, Suricata alerts 12 months, NetFlow 13 months. Which source answers the question?", options:["Suricata alerts, because they are kept for a full year","Proxy logs, because they record the user and the full URL","Full packet capture, because it contains every byte sent","NetFlow records, because they summarize every flow for 13 months"], answer:3, why:"NetFlow records who talked to whom, when and how much for every flow, and it is the only complete source kept for six months. Suricata alerts are retained long enough but exist only where a signature matched, so silent connections to the C2 address would be missed."},
 {id:"p21m2q4", q:"After a new firewall is onboarded, a correlation rule that joins on the CIM field src never fires for its traffic. The raw events show the address in a field called SourceAddr. What should you do first?", options:["Rewrite the rule's logic, because the attacker behavior it targets has changed","Fix the firewall add-on so SourceAddr maps to src, then confirm src fills","Widen the rule's time window so that slower correlations have time to match","Move the whole SIEM from CIM to OCSF so every product uses the same names"], answer:1, why:"Correlation works only when every source uses the same field name. The rule is fine; the parser never mapped SourceAddr onto src, so check the data before doubting the rule. Migrating schemas is a large project and would still need the same mapping."},
 {id:"p21m2q5", q:"You want the accounts with more than 50 failed logons in the selected time range. Which SPL search returns them?", options:["index=wineventlog EventCode=4625 | stats count by user | where count>50","index=wineventlog EventCode=4625 | where count>50 | stats count by user","index=wineventlog EventCode=4625 | stats dc(user) as count | where count>50","index=wineventlog EventCode=4625 | timechart count | where count>50"], answer:0, why:"stats must create the count field per user before where can filter on it. Putting where first filters on a field that does not exist yet, dc(user) returns a single number of distinct users, and timechart counts all failures per time bucket rather than per account."}
],
"p21m3":[
 {id:"p21m3q1", q:"Timeline: first malicious logon Monday 22:10 UTC; SIEM alert Tuesday 04:25; host isolated by EDR 04:55; service restored 13:25. What are the dwell time and the time to contain after detection?", options:["Dwell time 6 h 45 min; containment 30 min","Dwell time 6 h 15 min; containment 9 h","Dwell time 15 h 15 min; containment 30 min","Dwell time 6 h 15 min; containment 30 min"], answer:3, why:"Dwell time runs from the first attacker action to detection: 22:10 to 04:25 is 6 h 15 min. Containment is measured from detection to isolation, 04:25 to 04:55, so 30 minutes. Measuring dwell time up to isolation gives the tempting 6 h 45 min."},
 {id:"p21m3q2", q:"A phishing click with no credential entry was ticketed SEV3 at 10:00. At 12:10, EDR shows vssadmin delete shadows /all run under a service account on a file server. What is the correct handling?", options:["Keep it at SEV3, because severity is fixed when the incident is declared","Reassess to SEV1 at once and page the incident commander, legal and executives","Raise it to SEV2 and schedule containment for the next business day","Close the phishing ticket and open a separate SEV4 ticket for the file server alert"], answer:1, why:"Severity is reassessed as scope changes, and shadow-copy deletion on a file server is a ransomware precursor, which the severity table puts at SEV1 with executives and legal paged within 15 minutes. Severity is not frozen at declaration, and splitting the ticket hides the connection."},
 {id:"p21m3q3", q:"A workstation beacons to a C2 domain every 60 seconds. No encryption or data theft is visible yet, and legal wants evidence preserved. Which first containment step best fits NIST's containment criteria?", options:["Isolate it with EDR, keep it running for a memory capture, sinkhole the domain","Pull the power cord so the beacon stops immediately and nothing else can run on it","Reimage the workstation right away and return it to the user the same day","Scan the C2 server's address to map the rest of the attacker's infrastructure"], answer:0, why:"EDR isolation stops the attacker while memory, connections and a remote shell survive, and a DNS sinkhole cuts the channel fleet-wide. Pulling power destroys RAM evidence, reimaging destroys the scene, and scanning the attacker's server is hacking back outside your authority."},
 {id:"p21m3q4", q:"After a domain controller was touched, the team rebuilt the three infected servers from clean images, reset the phished user's password and closed the incident. Ten days later the same actor is back inside. What did eradication most likely miss?", options:["Capturing a memory image of each infected server before it was rebuilt","Rebuilding the servers from known-good images rather than cleaning them in place","Rotating exposed credentials (KRBTGT twice) and sweeping hosts for persistence","Telling customers and the press before the systems were back in service"], answer:2, why:"Rebuilding three servers removes symptoms, not the cause. With a domain controller touched, every credential the attacker could read, including service accounts and KRBTGT (twice), must rotate, and every host needs a persistence sweep. Memory capture serves evidence but does not stop re-entry."},
 {id:"p21m3q5", q:"A US public company's legal team determines on a Thursday that a cyber incident is material. Under SEC Form 8-K Item 1.05, when is the disclosure due?", options:["72 hours after the security team first detected the intrusion","Four business days after the intrusion itself first began","60 days after the determination, as for HIPAA individual notices","Four business days after the determination: the next Wednesday"], answer:3, why:"The 8-K clock starts when the company determines the incident is material, not when it began or was detected. Counting business days from Thursday gives Friday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday. The 72-hour figure belongs to GDPR, and 60 days to HIPAA's individual notices."}
],
"p21m4":[
 {id:"p21m4q1", q:"You must image a 4 TB drive that reads at a sustained 200 MB/s, then verify the image by hashing it again at the same rate. Roughly how long should you budget for the whole job?", options:["About 11 hours: 5.6 to acquire and 5.6 to verify","About 5.6 hours in total for acquisition and verification","About 2.8 hours for acquisition and verification together","About 22 hours: 11 to acquire and 11 to verify"], answer:0, why:"4,000,000 MB ÷ 200 MB/s = 20,000 s, about 5.6 hours to read the drive, and verification reads it again, so about 11 hours in total. Forgetting the verification pass gives the 5.6-hour figure, which is how acquisitions overrun their window."},
 {id:"p21m4q2", q:"A Chrome History row stores visit_time = 13,400,000,000,000,000 in WebKit time (microseconds since 1601-01-01). What is the Unix timestamp in seconds?", options:["13,400,000,000","25,044,473,600","1,755,526,400","1,755,526,400,000"], answer:2, why:"Divide by 1,000,000 to get seconds since 1601 (13,400,000,000), then subtract the 11,644,473,600-second offset to 1970, giving 1,755,526,400, which is 2025-08-18 14:13:20 UTC. Stopping after the division leaves a 1601-based value, and adding the offset moves the date the wrong way."},
 {id:"p21m4q3", q:"An insider case needs the number of bytes one application sent over the network, hour by hour, last Tuesday. The laptop's network had no proxy or flow logging. Which artifact answers it?", options:["Prefetch, which records each program's last eight run times","SRUM (SRUDB.dat), which records per-application bytes sent and received by hour","Shellbags, which record the folders a user browsed in Explorer","Amcache.hve, which records executables with their SHA-1 hash and first-seen time"], answer:1, why:"SRUM keeps per-application network bytes sent and received in hourly buckets, which is how the lesson quantifies exfiltration. Prefetch proves a program ran and when, but says nothing about how much data it moved; shellbags and Amcache say nothing about the network."},
 {id:"p21m4q4", q:"Reviewing windows.pstree output from a Windows memory image, which entry is the strongest sign of compromise?", options:["svchost.exe with parent services.exe, image path C:\\Windows\\System32","lsass.exe, a single instance, with parent wininit.exe","explorer.exe whose parent userinit.exe has already exited","svchost.exe with parent explorer.exe, running from a user's Temp folder"], answer:3, why:"Legitimate svchost.exe is started by services.exe from System32, so one launched by explorer.exe from a Temp folder is a masquerading binary. A single lsass under wininit and an explorer.exe whose userinit parent has exited are both normal Windows behavior."},
 {id:"p21m4q5", q:"You find a running, unlocked laptop with BitLocker enabled, and legal has authorized collection. Which sequence preserves the most evidence?", options:["Capture RAM, copy the mounted volume, get the recovery key from AD or Intune, then image","Pull the power at once to freeze the scene, then image the drive behind a hardware write blocker","Image the physical disk first while the system runs, then capture memory at the end","Run an antivirus scan to remove the malware first, then capture memory and image"], answer:0, why:"The order of volatility puts RAM first, and while the machine is unlocked the decrypted volume and keys are available. Pulling power loses memory and leaves an encrypted image you may not be able to read without the recovery key; scanning first alters the evidence."}
],
"p21m5":[
 {id:"p21m5q1", q:"An email attachment named invoice.pdf begins with the bytes 50 4B 03 04. What is it, and how should triage proceed?", options:["A normal PDF, since 50 4B is the PDF signature; open it in a reader","A Windows executable, since 50 4B is the MZ header; detonate it at once","A ZIP container, as .docx and .jar are; inspect it as an archive in the lab","A Linux ELF binary; triage it on REMnux rather than on FLARE VM"], answer:2, why:"50 4B 03 04 is the ZIP signature, shared by Office Open XML files and Java archives; a real PDF starts 25 50 44 46 (%PDF) and a PE starts 4D 5A (MZ). The file is not what its name claims, so inspect its contents inside the isolated lab."},
 {id:"p21m5q2", q:"pestudio shows an unsigned sample importing VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory and CreateRemoteThread. Which capability does that import set most strongly indicate?", options:["Persistence through a Run registry key","File encryption, as ransomware uses","Anti-analysis through debugger checks","Code injection into another process"], answer:3, why:"Allocating memory in another process, writing into it and starting a thread there is the textbook process-injection sequence. Run-key persistence would show RegSetValue calls, ransomware CryptEncrypt with file enumeration, and anti-analysis calls such as IsDebuggerPresent."},
 {id:"p21m5q3", q:"A binary carved from memory contains, in its configuration, what looks like your company's customer records. You want to know whether anyone else has seen it. What is the right first move?", options:["Upload the file to VirusTotal so every vendor engine can analyze it","Look up its SHA-256 on VirusTotal without uploading the file","Rename the file and upload it so the adversary cannot recognize it","Submit it to a public sandbox, which keeps uploaded files confidential"], answer:1, why:"A hash lookup tells you whether the sample is known without sharing it. Uploading shares the file, and the company data inside it, with every vendor and can tip off the adversary. Renaming changes nothing, because the hash, not the name, identifies the file."},
 {id:"p21m5q4", q:"Detonation shows a sample beaconing every 120 seconds with 20% jitter. Which inter-arrival range should your hunt expect, and which property of the deltas singles the beacon out?", options:["96–144 s; a standard deviation small relative to the mean","100–140 s; many distinct destinations per source host","96–144 s; a standard deviation larger than the mean","120–144 s; the largest total bytes sent per source host"], answer:0, why:"20% of 120 s is 24 s, so intervals fall between 96 and 144 s. Machine timing stays tight around the mean (here the standard deviation is roughly 12% of it), unlike human browsing, which is why the hunt filters for low variance, not high."},
 {id:"p21m5q5", q:"A Sigma rule for Office spawning script interpreters fires daily on a finance team's approved Excel macro. A colleague adds a filter excluding every event whose ParentImage ends with \\EXCEL.EXE. What is wrong with that fix?", options:["Nothing; excluding a noisy parent process is the standard way to tune it","Sigma filters cannot reference ParentImage, so the whole rule will fail to convert to SPL","It blinds the rule to all Excel-launched attacks; allowlist the macro's command line","The level should be lowered to low instead, leaving the logic exactly as is"], answer:2, why:"Tuning should remove the known-good case and nothing else. Excluding the Excel parent removes one of the main attack paths the rule exists to catch; filtering on the approved macro's command line, as the lesson's filter_addin does, keeps coverage while removing the noise."}
],
"p21m6":[
 {id:"p21m6q1", q:"Compute the CVSS v3.1 base score for CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H (an authenticated remote code execution).", options:["9.8 (Critical)","8.8 (High)","8.7 (High)","9.0 (Critical)"], answer:1, why:"ISS = 0.9148 and Impact = 6.42 × 0.9148 = 5.873; Exploitability = 8.22 × 0.85 × 0.77 × 0.62 × 0.85 = 2.835; the sum 8.708 rounds up to 8.8. Using 0.68 (the PR:L value for Scope Changed) gives 9.0, and rounding down gives 8.7."},
 {id:"p21m6q2", q:"Read this vector: CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:P/VC:H/VI:L/VA:N/SC:H/SI:N/SA:N. Which statement is correct?", options:["A passive user interaction is needed, and a subsequent system suffers high confidentiality impact","Scope is Changed, so the formula multiplies the impact plus exploitability sum by 1.08","Special attack requirements must be present, and the vulnerable system loses all of its availability","No user interaction is needed, and only the vulnerable system itself is affected at all"], answer:0, why:"UI:P is Passive interaction, and SC:H is a high confidentiality impact on a subsequent system beyond the vulnerable one. CVSS v4.0 has no Scope metric and no 1.08 formula; it scores by MacroVector lookup. AT:N means no attack requirements, and VA:N means no availability impact."},
 {id:"p21m6q3", q:"A file server is valued at $800,000 with an exposure factor of 40% and an ARO of 0.25. A control costing $60,000 a year would cut the ARO to 0.1. Is the control cost-justified on ALE alone?", options:["Yes: it removes $80,000 of annual loss, more than it costs","Yes: ALE falls from $200,000 to $80,000, saving $120,000 a year","Yes: SLE falls from $320,000 to $128,000, saving $192,000","No: ALE falls by $48,000 a year, less than the $60,000 it costs"], answer:3, why:"SLE = $800,000 × 0.40 = $320,000. ALE = $320,000 × 0.25 = $80,000 now and $320,000 × 0.1 = $32,000 with the control, a $48,000 saving for $60,000. Claiming the full $80,000 ignores the residual ALE that remains."},
 {id:"p21m6q4", q:"A clinic has infusion pumps that crash under active probes, Linux servers, and staff laptops that are rarely on the office network. Which scanning plan fits each asset class?", options:["Unauthenticated active scans of everything weekly, since that is what an attacker sees","Credentialed active scans of the pumps with safe checks off, and agents on the servers","Passive monitoring for the pumps, credentialed scans for the servers, agents on the laptops","Agents on the pumps, passive monitoring for the laptops, unauthenticated scans for servers"], answer:2, why:"Passive scanners watch traffic, so fragile medical devices are never probed; credentialed scans read installed packages on servers accurately; agents cover laptops that never sit on the office network. Weekly unauthenticated scans would crash the pumps and miss most local patches."},
 {id:"p21m6q5", q:"An unauthenticated scan flags a Debian server's OpenSSH version banner as vulnerable to a CVE. A credentialed scan shows the distribution's backported fix is installed. What is the right call?", options:["Upgrade to the newest upstream OpenSSH anyway, since the version banner is always authoritative","Record a banner false positive, keep the evidence, and scan that host class credentialed","Formally accept the risk for twelve months, with the server's owner as the signer","Drop the server from future scans, because the scanner has proven unreliable"], answer:1, why:"Backported patches leave the version string unchanged, so banner-based findings on distribution packages are a known false-positive source, and the credentialed evidence line settles it. Risk acceptance is for real vulnerabilities you cannot fix, not for findings that are not real."}
],
"p21m7":[
 {id:"p21m7q1", q:"A company has 600 hosts on one flat network. It moves them into six VLANs of 100 hosts each, with default-deny firewall rules between VLANs. Ignoring the few allowed services, how many hosts can a phished laptop reach directly now?", options:["0 now, down from 599, because default-deny blocks everything","100 now, down from 600 before","594 now, since VLANs do not limit reach","99 now, down from 599 before"], answer:3, why:"Inside its own VLAN of 100 the laptop has 99 neighbors, versus 599 on the flat network, a sixfold cut. Default-deny filters traffic between VLANs, not within one, so the count is not zero; reaching other zones now means crossing a logged, filtered boundary."},
 {id:"p21m7q2", q:"Remote staff use a full-tunnel VPN. A phished laptop on the VPN reached the domain controller because VPN users are treated as insiders. Which change follows NIST SP 800-207 zero trust?", options:["Upgrade the VPN cipher suite to AES-256 and lengthen the session timeout","Add SMS one-time codes to the VPN login and keep the full tunnel in place","Replace the VPN with per-app ZTNA that checks identity, device health and context","Move the VPN concentrator into the DMZ so all traffic passes the perimeter firewall"], answer:2, why:"Zero trust removes trust based on network location: a policy decision point evaluates identity, device health and context for each resource, and ZTNA brokers per-application access. SMS codes add MFA but can be relayed, and they still leave the laptop on the network."},
 {id:"p21m7q3", q:"Which egress policy for a server zone does most to stop command-and-control traffic and data exfiltration?", options:["Default-deny outbound; servers reach only internal DNS resolvers and the proxy","Allow all outbound TCP 80 and 443 for updates and deny every other destination port","Allow outbound DNS to any public resolver so updates always resolve","Filter inbound traffic only, since outbound connections start inside"], answer:0, why:"Most C2 and exfiltration ride ordinary web or DNS traffic, so servers should have no direct outbound path, only logged internal resolvers and the proxy. Allowing all 80 and 443 leaves exactly the channel most C2 uses, and open outbound DNS invites tunneling."},
 {id:"p21m7q4", q:"Mail with From: ceo@example.com, sent from attacker infrastructure with Return-Path bounce@attacker.net, passes SPF and is delivered. example.com publishes DMARC p=none. Why did it arrive, and what fixes it?", options:["SPF checked the visible From header and passed; harden example.com's SPF to -all","SPF passed for attacker.net, which fails DMARC alignment, and p=none only monitors","DMARC needs a published BIMI record and verified logo before it can take effect","example.com's SPF exceeds ten DNS lookups; flatten the record so it evaluates"], answer:1, why:"SPF checks the envelope sender, so it passed for attacker.net, which does not align with the visible From domain, and DMARC failed. With p=none, receivers only report. Protection arrives at quarantine and then reject, once the aggregate reports confirm every legitimate sender."},
 {id:"p21m7q5", q:"A 9 TB file server has a 6-hour RTO. What sustained throughput does the restore need, and can it come from a single cloud copy over a 1 Gbps link?", options:["About 0.4 Gbps; yes, a 1 Gbps link finishes in about 2.5 hours","About 1.5 Gbps; no, a 1 Gbps link needs about 9 hours","About 27 Gbps; no link could meet it, so the RTO must change","About 3.3 Gbps; no, a 1 Gbps link needs about 20 hours"], answer:3, why:"9 TB ÷ 6 h = 1.5 TB/h, about 417 MB/s or 3.3 Gbps. At 1 Gbps (125 MB/s), 9 TB takes 72,000 s, about 20 hours, so the design needs a local immutable copy or parallel streams. The 0.4 figure mistakes megabytes per second for megabits."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p22m1":[
 {id:"p22m1q1", q:"Install times have Q1 = 40 min and Q3 = 70 min. Using Tukey's fences, which of these readings is flagged for a closer look?", options:["100 min, because it sits more than half an IQR above Q3","3 min, because it sits more than one IQR below Q1","118 min, because it lies above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 115","112 min, because it lies more than 1.5 × IQR above the median"], answer:2, why:"IQR = 70 − 40 = 30, so the fences are 40 − 45 = −5 and 70 + 45 = 115 minutes, and only 118 falls outside. The rule uses the quartiles and 1.5 × IQR, not the median or a single IQR, and a flagged value is investigated, not deleted."},
 {id:"p22m1q2", q:"Crew leads tend to leave the 'rework needed' field blank when a splice failed its retest, and fill it in when all went well. How should you classify the blanks, and what happens if you drop those rows?", options:["Missing not at random; dropping them makes the rework rate look lower than it is","Missing completely at random; dropping them leaves the rework rate unbiased","Missing at random, explained by the form version; impute from the form version field","Not missing at all; a blank means no rework, so fill each one with a zero"], answer:0, why:"Whether the value is missing depends on the value itself (failures go unrecorded), which is missing not at random. Dropping those rows removes failures preferentially and biases the rate downward. Treating blanks as zero makes the same mistake; the honest fix is to find out why."},
 {id:"p22m1q3", q:"Crew A logged 21 rework splices out of 3,000 made; Crew B logged 9 out of 600. Which statement is correct?", options:["Crew A has the worse rework rate, because 21 is more than twice 9","The two crews have about the same rework rate, roughly 1% each","Crew A has the worse rate: 7% against Crew B's 1.5%","Crew B has the worse rework rate: 1.5% against Crew A's 0.7%"], answer:3, why:"A count without its denominator is a rumor: 21 ÷ 3,000 = 0.7% and 9 ÷ 600 = 1.5%, so Crew B's rate is about twice Crew A's. Comparing raw counts rewards the crew that did less work."},
 {id:"p22m1q4", q:"An export has columns crew, Jan, Feb and Mar, each month holding feet placed. You need a pivot table and a Power BI line chart by month. What should you do first?", options:["Pivot it wider so that each crew also becomes its own column","Unpivot (melt) it into three columns: crew, month and feet","Merge the three month headers into one cell labeled Q1","Add a Total column and color the best month green"], answer:1, why:"Month is a variable, so tidy data puts it in one column, with one row per crew-month observation. Pivot tables, pandas and Power BI all expect that long shape; widening makes it worse, and merged cells and color-as-data break every downstream tool."},
 {id:"p22m1q5", q:"A splice-defect detector catches 99% of defects and falsely flags 1% of good splices. One splice in 2,000 is truly defective. Across 100,000 splices, roughly how many flags are false, and what share of flags are real defects?", options:["About 1 false flag; about 99% of flags are real","About 50 false flags; about half of the flags are real","About 1,000 false flags; only about 5% of flags are real","About 1,000 false flags; about 95% of flags are real"], answer:2, why:"There are 50 defects, nearly all caught, and 1% of the 99,950 good splices, about 1,000, are falsely flagged. So roughly 50 of 1,050 flags, about 5%, are real. The 99% figure describes the detector, not its flags, when the base rate is low."}
],
"p22m2":[
 {id:"p22m2q1", q:"Roughly how much memory do 5 million float64 values take as a NumPy array, compared with a Python list of the same floats (about 24 bytes per float object plus an 8-byte pointer each)?", options:["About 5 MB as an array versus about 40 MB as a list","About 40 MB as an array versus about 160 MB as a list","About 40 MB either way, since a float is 8 bytes in both","About 160 MB as an array versus about 40 MB as a list"], answer:1, why:"The array is one contiguous block: 5,000,000 × 8 bytes = 40 MB. The list holds 5 million separate float objects of about 24 bytes plus 8-byte pointers, roughly 32 bytes per value, so about 160 MB, scattered across memory and slower to loop over."},
 {id:"p22m2q2", q:"In pandas 3.0, df[df.loss_db > 0.3]['flag'] = True runs without changing df. Which line sets the flag correctly?", options:["df.loc[df.loss_db > 0.3, 'flag'] = True","df[df.loss_db > 0.3].flag = True","df.iloc[df.loss_db > 0.3, 'flag'] = True","df.copy()[df.loss_db > 0.3]['flag'] = True"], answer:0, why:"Under Copy-on-Write every selection behaves as a copy, so chained assignment writes into a temporary object. A single .loc call with a mask and a column label modifies df itself. iloc takes integer positions, not a column name, and the copy() version still assigns into a copy."},
 {id:"p22m2q3", q:"A CSV column of whole-number splice counts has three blank cells. With default settings, what dtype does pd.read_csv give that column?", options:["int64, with the three blanks stored as 0","object, because the column now mixes types","str, the pandas 3.0 default for any column","float64, because NaN is a floating-point value"], answer:3, why:"pandas marks blanks as NaN, and NaN is a float, so an integer column with any missing value is read as float64. Blanks are never silently turned into zero, and the str default applies to text columns, not to numbers."},
 {id:"p22m2q4", q:"You merge 5,000 installs onto a crew table on crew_id and want the merge to fail loudly if any crew_id repeats in the crew table, rather than silently inflating totals. Which call does that?", options:["pd.merge(installs, crews, on='crew_id', how='inner')","pd.merge(installs, crews, on='crew_id', how='left', indicator=True)","pd.merge(installs, crews, on='crew_id', how='left', validate='many_to_one')","pd.merge(installs, crews, on='crew_id', how='left', validate='one_to_many')"], answer:2, why:"validate='many_to_one' asserts that the right-hand key is unique and raises if it is not. indicator=True only labels which rows matched, inner changes which rows are kept, and one_to_many would wrongly demand that installs, which repeat crew_id, be unique."},
 {id:"p22m2q5", q:"A frame has one row per install with columns crew, date and feet, and crews log several installs a day. Which line gives each crew's total feet and the number of distinct days it worked?", options:["df.groupby('crew').agg(total=('feet','sum'), days=('date','count'))","df.groupby('crew').agg(total=('feet','sum'), days=('date','nunique'))","df.groupby('date').agg(total=('feet','sum'), days=('crew','nunique'))","df.agg(total=('feet','sum'), days=('date','nunique')).groupby('crew')"], answer:1, why:"Grouping by crew splits the rows per crew, sum totals the feet, and nunique counts distinct dates. count would count install rows, overstating days whenever a crew logs several installs in one day, and grouping by date answers a different question."}
],
"p22m3":[
 {id:"p22m3q1", q:"You need to show whether weekly help-desk ticket volume changed after a new self-service portal launched, using 52 weeks of data. Which chart fits the question?", options:["A line chart of weekly tickets, with the launch date annotated","A pie chart with one slice for each of the 52 weeks of tickets","A histogram showing the spread of the 52 weekly ticket counts","A choropleth map of tickets by customer state for the year"], answer:0, why:"Change over time calls for a line with time on the x-axis and the event marked on the chart. A histogram shows how weekly counts are distributed but throws away their order, so it cannot show a before-and-after shift; pies and maps answer other questions."},
 {id:"p22m3q2", q:"A bubble chart shows revenue doubling by doubling the bubble's diameter. Using Tufte's lie factor (size of the visual change divided by size of the data change), how large is the distortion?", options:["1, because the diameter doubled exactly as the data did","2, because the radius doubled along with the data","4, because the new bubble has four times the area","3, because the area grew 300% while the data grew 100%"], answer:3, why:"Readers judge a bubble by its area, and doubling the diameter quadruples the area: a 300% visual increase against a 100% data increase, so the lie factor is 300 ÷ 100 = 3. Four is the ratio of the areas, not the ratio of the changes."},
 {id:"p22m3q3", q:"A route dashboard marks on-target routes green and off-target routes red, with no other cue. What is the best fix?", options:["Make the red and the green brighter so they contrast more","Add a legend explaining what red and green each mean","Use a colorblind-safe palette and add a second cue, such as a label","Switch to a rainbow palette so that each route has its own hue"], answer:2, why:"About 8% of men have red-green color-vision deficiency, so hue alone fails for roughly one male reader in twelve. Brightness and a legend do not restore the distinction; a safe palette plus a redundant channel (label, shape or position) does."},
 {id:"p22m3q4", q:"A slide shows cumulative customer sign-ups rising every month, captioned 'strong growth'. The monthly new sign-ups behind it have fallen 60% since spring. What is wrong, and what should be shown?", options:["Nothing is wrong; a line that keeps rising proves that growth is continuing strongly","A cumulative chart can only rise, hiding the collapse; plot monthly new sign-ups","The y-axis should be logarithmic so that the late months look steeper","It should be a 3D area chart so that the size of the total is clearer"], answer:1, why:"A running total rises as long as anyone signs up, so it hides a collapsing rate. The decision depends on the monthly rate, which fell 60%. A log axis or a 3D area would distort the picture further rather than reveal it."},
 {id:"p22m3q5", q:"Which title best follows the storytelling rules for a chart of monthly churn presented to an executive?", options:["Churn fell from 4.1% to 2.6% after the May onboarding change","Monthly churn rate by plan, January to December 2026","Churn analysis: see the appendix for the full details","Figure 3: customer churn by month and by plan type"], answer:0, why:"Executives read the title and the first number, so the title should state the conclusion with its magnitude. The other options name the variable or point elsewhere, leaving the reader to find the message, which fails the five-second test."}
],
"p22m4":[
 {id:"p22m4q1", q:"Fit y = a + bx by least squares to the points x = 1, 2, 3, 4 and y = 10, 14, 15, 21. What is the fitted line?", options:["y = 3.4 + 6.5x","y = 15 + 3.4x","y = 6.5 + 3.4x","y = −27.5 + 17x"], answer:2, why:"x̄ = 2.5 and ȳ = 15; the cross-products sum to 7.5 + 0.5 + 0 + 9 = 17 and Σ(x − x̄)² = 5, so b = 3.4 and a = 15 − 3.4 × 2.5 = 6.5. Using 17 without dividing by 5 gives the last option; 15 + 3.4x forgets to subtract b × x̄."},
 {id:"p22m4q2", q:"A sample of 900 splices has mean loss 0.060 dB and standard deviation 0.030 dB. What is the 95% confidence interval for the mean loss?", options:["0.059 to 0.061 dB","0.058 to 0.062 dB","0.001 to 0.119 dB","0.0599 to 0.0601 dB"], answer:1, why:"SE = 0.030 ÷ √900 = 0.001 dB, and 1.96 × 0.001 ≈ 0.002, so 0.058 to 0.062 dB. ±1 SE gives only a 68% interval, ±1.96 SD describes individual splices rather than the mean, and dividing by n instead of √n makes the interval far too narrow."},
 {id:"p22m4q3", q:"Control converts 200 of 2,000 users (10.0%); the variant converts 250 of 2,000 (12.5%). Using a pooled two-proportion z-test, what is the result?", options:["z ≈ 2.5, p ≈ 0.01: significant at α = 0.05","z ≈ 1.77, p ≈ 0.08: not significant, as with 1,000 per arm","z ≈ 1.25, p ≈ 0.21: not significant at α = 0.05","z ≈ 25, p < 0.0001: overwhelmingly significant"], answer:0, why:"Pooled p = 450 ÷ 4,000 = 0.1125; SE = √(0.1125 × 0.8875 × (1/2000 + 1/2000)) ≈ 0.0100; z = 0.025 ÷ 0.0100 ≈ 2.5, two-sided p ≈ 0.012. The same rates on 1,000 per arm gave z = 1.77; doubling n shrinks the SE by √2."},
 {id:"p22m4q4", q:"An A/B test with 50/50 hash assignment has 31,000 users so far: 16,400 in control and 14,600 in the variant. What should you conclude?", options:["The variant is driving users away, so stop the test and roll back","Nothing is wrong; random assignment always drifts by a few percent at this size","Discard 1,800 control users at random to rebalance, then analyze","The 53/47 split means assignment is broken; fix it before reading results"], answer:3, why:"A fair split of 31,000 would land within a few hundred of 15,500 each; a 900-user gap is about 10 standard deviations off, so assignment or logging is broken. Results from a broken split cannot be interpreted, and discarding users does not repair the bias."},
 {id:"p22m4q5", q:"Across 200 towns, those with more fiber installs have higher median household income (r = 0.8). An analyst writes that fiber raises incomes. What is the strongest objection?", options:["None; a correlation of 0.8 is strong enough to establish causation","Builds go first to high-income towns, so causation may run the other way","r = 0.8 means fiber explains 80% of the variation in income","Simpson's paradox cannot apply, so the claim needs only a p-value"], answer:1, why:"Correlation is symmetric: high-income towns attract builds (reverse causation), and density or demand can drive both (confounding). Only randomization or a careful quasi-experiment earns the word causes. Also, r = 0.8 gives r² = 0.64, so 64% of variance, not 80%."}
],
"p22m5":[
 {id:"p22m5q1", q:"A churn model is run on 2,000 customers, 200 of whom churn. It flags 150, and 90 of those churn. What are its precision, recall and F1?", options:["Precision 45%, recall 60%, F1 ≈ 0.51","Precision 60%, recall 75%, F1 ≈ 0.67","Precision 60%, recall 45%, F1 ≈ 0.53","Precision 60%, recall 45%, F1 ≈ 0.51"], answer:3, why:"Precision = 90 ÷ 150 = 60% of flags correct; recall = 90 ÷ 200 = 45% of churners caught. F1 is the harmonic mean, 2 × 0.6 × 0.45 ÷ 1.05 ≈ 0.51; 0.53 is the plain average, which overstates a lopsided pair."},
 {id:"p22m5q2", q:"A churn model scores AUC 0.99 in cross-validation but 0.62 in production. One feature is has_cancellation_ticket, created when a customer asks to close the account. What is the diagnosis?", options:["Leakage: the feature exists only once churn is under way, so drop it and retrain","Underfitting: the model needs more trees and deeper splits to generalize to production","Scaling: the production data needs its own StandardScaler fitted on it","Wrong metric: switch from AUC to accuracy, which is more stable"], answer:0, why:"A feature that appears only as the outcome happens would not exist at prediction time, so validation was flattered by information from the future, and the honest score is the production one. Fitting a scaler on production data is itself leakage, and accuracy hides class imbalance."},
 {id:"p22m5q3", q:"Two features are income in dollars (tens of thousands) and visits per month (single digits). For which model does skipping StandardScaler most change the result?", options:["A random forest classifier","A histogram gradient-boosting model","k-means clustering","A single decision tree"], answer:2, why:"k-means groups points by distance, so unscaled income would swamp visits and decide every cluster. Trees, forests and boosting split one feature at a time on thresholds and are unaffected by rescaling, which is why scaling them changes nothing."},
 {id:"p22m5q4", q:"A regression model's errors on four test rows are 1, −1, 2 and −6. What are its MAE and RMSE?", options:["MAE −1.0 and RMSE ≈ 3.24","MAE 2.5 and RMSE ≈ 3.24","MAE 2.5 and RMSE 2.5","MAE 2.5 and RMSE 10.5"], answer:1, why:"MAE averages absolute errors: (1 + 1 + 2 + 6) ÷ 4 = 2.5. RMSE squares first: (1 + 1 + 4 + 36) ÷ 4 = 10.5, and √10.5 ≈ 3.24, larger because it punishes the 6-unit miss. The signed mean is −1.0, and 10.5 forgets the square root."},
 {id:"p22m5q5", q:"You are building a model to forecast daily ticket volume from two years of history. How should you validate it?", options:["A random 80/20 split of all days, stratified by weekday","Shuffle the days and run 10-fold cross-validation for a tight estimate","Tune hyperparameters on the most recent months, used as the test set","Train on earlier periods and validate on later ones, keeping time order"], answer:3, why:"Time-ordered data must be split by time, or the model learns from days after the ones it predicts and the score is inflated. Shuffled splits and shuffled cross-validation leak the future, and tuning on the test set spends the one honest estimate."}
],
"p22m6":[
 {id:"p22m6q1", q:"Which crontab schedule runs a Power BI export script at 05:30 on weekdays only?", options:["5 30 * * 1-5","30 5 1-5 * *","30 5 * * 1-5","*/30 5 * * 1-5"], answer:2, why:"The fields are minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, so 30 5 * * 1-5 is 05:30 Monday to Friday. Swapping the first two fields is invalid, 1-5 in the third field means the 1st to 5th of the month, and */30 runs at 05:00 and 05:30."},
 {id:"p22m6q2", q:"A nightly job appends the day's rows to the fact table with INSERT. It failed partway at 02:40, was rerun at 09:00, and the dashboard now shows that day's footage almost doubled. What is the right fix?", options:["Make the load idempotent: replace the day's partition, or upsert on the key","Add automatic retries so that the first run is far less likely to fail","Move the job from cron to Airflow, which removes duplicate rows itself","Switch Power BI to DirectQuery so the report reads the source directly"], answer:0, why:"Rerunning a day must produce the same result, so the load should replace that day's partition or upsert on the key. Retries still append duplicates on any rerun, and Airflow schedules and retries tasks but does not make a load idempotent."},
 {id:"p22m6q3", q:"A fact table has 200 million rows and 25 columns of 8 bytes each. A query sums two columns. Before compression, how much does a row store read compared with a column store?", options:["40 GB in both, since every row must be scanned","5 GB versus 0.4 GB","400 GB versus 32 GB","40 GB versus 3.2 GB"], answer:3, why:"Row storage reads whole rows: 200,000,000 × 25 × 8 bytes = 40 GB. Columnar storage reads only the two needed columns: 200,000,000 × 2 × 8 = 3.2 GB, and compression typically cuts that 5–10× further. Forgetting the 8 bytes per value gives 5 GB and 0.4 GB."},
 {id:"p22m6q4", q:"Tonight's extract returns 0.9 million rows. The 7-day median is 2.4 million, and the validation allows ±30%. What should the pipeline do?", options:["Load the rows anyway, since partial fresh data beats stale data","Fail the check, alert a human and stop, leaving yesterday's tables intact","Load them, then compare the totals by hand at the morning meeting","Keep retrying every 15 minutes until the row count is back in range, then load it"], answer:1, why:"0.9 million is far below the lower bound of 1.68 million (2.4 × 0.7), so something upstream is broken. A pipeline that loads garbage on time is the worst failure because nobody looks; fail loudly and keep known-good data in place. Silent retries hide the problem."},
 {id:"p22m6q5", q:"Supervisors must see only their own crews' rows in one shared Power BI report. Which approach enforces that?", options:["A supervisor slicer on each page, defaulted to the viewer's own name","A separate copy of the report for each supervisor, emailed weekly","An RLS role filtering crews on [SupervisorEmail] = USERPRINCIPALNAME()","Hiding the crew and supervisor columns in every visual of the report"], answer:2, why:"Row-level security filters the model itself for each signed-in user, and the filter propagates from the crew dimension to the facts. A slicer is a convenience any viewer can change, hidden columns leave the data in the model, and emailed copies multiply versions."}
],
"p22m7":[
 {id:"p22m7q1", q:"Which opening sentence of a one-page report best follows BLUF?", options:["This report describes the data sources, methods and analyses used in Q3.","Backlog fell 56% (410 to 180) after adding a night shift; we recommend keeping it.","After extensive analysis of the data, several interesting trends in the backlog emerged.","Section 1 introduces the backlog; the findings follow later, in Section 4."], answer:1, why:"Bottom line up front means the answer, its magnitude and the ask come first, so the decision maker could act after one sentence. The other openings describe the document or defer the finding, which is exactly what executives skip past."},
 {id:"p22m7q2", q:"A screening model selects 60% of group A applicants and 42% of group B applicants. What does the four-fifths rule say?", options:["The gap is 18 points, under 20, so no adverse impact is flagged","The ratio is 1.43, above 0.80, so no adverse impact is flagged","The ratio is 0.70, above 0.50, so no adverse impact is flagged","The ratio is 0.70, below 0.80, so adverse impact is flagged"], answer:3, why:"The rule compares each group's selection rate with the highest group's as a ratio: 42 ÷ 60 = 0.70, below 0.80, which is evidence of adverse impact. It is not a percentage-point gap, and inverting the ratio (60 ÷ 42) hides the problem."},
 {id:"p22m7q3", q:"A clinic wants to share a dataset under HIPAA Safe Harbor. It holds year of birth (all patients are under 80), state, 5-digit ZIP code, visit month and diagnosis code; names and record numbers are gone. Which fields must still be removed or generalized?", options:["The 5-digit ZIP code and the visit month","Only the diagnosis code, since it is health data","Only the year of birth, since it reveals age","None, since names and record numbers are gone"], answer:0, why:"Safe Harbor removes geography below state level and every date element except the year, so the 5-digit ZIP and the visit month must go or be generalized. Year of birth and state are allowed, and the diagnosis is the health content the dataset exists to share."},
 {id:"p22m7q4", q:"A loan model correctly approves 70% of applicants who would repay in both group X and group Y (equal recall), but approves 50% of all group X applicants and 30% of group Y. Which fairness definitions does it meet?", options:["It meets demographic parity but violates equal opportunity","It meets both demographic parity and equal opportunity","It meets equal opportunity but violates demographic parity","It violates both, because the approval rates differ"], answer:2, why:"Equal opportunity asks for equal recall across groups, which holds at 70%. Demographic parity asks for equal positive rates, which fails at 50% versus 30%. When base rates differ these definitions conflict, so a model card should report both by subgroup."},
 {id:"p22m7q5", q:"A colleague clones your repository, runs the notebook and gets AUC 0.912 instead of your 0.927, plus an error in one pandas cell. Which fix addresses both problems?", options:["Commit the notebook with its outputs so the colleague can see your numbers","Pin the environment in a lock file, seed every random step, pass Restart-and-run-all","Ask the colleague to run the cells in exactly the same order that you originally ran them","Enlarge the test set until the AUC stops changing between the two runs"], answer:1, why:"Different library versions (pandas 3 is not pandas 2) explain the error, and unseeded splits or models explain the drift. A lock file, seeds and a clean top-to-bottom run make the result reproducible; committed outputs only show numbers nobody can regenerate."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p23m1":[
 {id:"p23m1q1", q:"A 470 Ω resistor rated ¼ W is connected directly across a 12 V supply. What current flows, and what happens to the resistor?", options:["About 25.5 mA and 0.31 W, over its ¼ W rating: use a ½ W part","About 2.55 mA and 0.03 W, well inside its ¼ W rating","About 25.5 mA and 0.31 W, fine because ratings include a 50% margin","About 12.8 mA and 0.15 W, comfortably inside its ¼ W rating"], answer:0, why:"I = 12 ÷ 470 = 25.5 mA and P = V² ÷ R = 144 ÷ 470 = 0.31 W, which exceeds 0.25 W, so the part overheats. A power rating is a limit to stay well below, not a figure with hidden margin; a ½ W or 1 W resistor is the fix."},
 {id:"p23m1q2", q:"A divider feeds a 12 V signal to an ADC: R1 = 10 kΩ from 12 V to the output, R2 = 4.7 kΩ from the output to ground. What is the output, and what does connecting a 10 MΩ ADC input do to it?", options:["About 8.16 V, and the 10 MΩ input barely changes it","About 5.64 V, and the 10 MΩ input halves it","About 3.84 V, and the 10 MΩ input pulls it close to zero","About 3.84 V, and the 10 MΩ input barely changes it"], answer:3, why:"Vout = 12 × 4.7 ÷ (10 + 4.7) ≈ 3.84 V. 10 MΩ in parallel with 4.7 kΩ is still about 4.7 kΩ, so the reading barely moves; 8.16 V is the drop across R1. A divider collapses only under a load that draws real current, like the lesson's 220 Ω sensor."},
 {id:"p23m1q3", q:"At one node you measure 120 mA flowing in from the supply, and 45 mA and 50 mA flowing out through the only two loads on your schematic. What does Kirchhoff's current law tell you?", options:["Nothing useful; KCL allows a node to lose some current as heat","Charge is building up at the node at a rate of 25 mA","About 25 mA leaves by an undrawn path, such as a leak or a short","The loads must be in series, so their currents should not be added"], answer:2, why:"KCL says the currents into a node equal the currents out, and charge cannot pile up. 120 − 45 − 50 = 25 mA must be leaving somewhere you have not drawn: a leak, a short or a forgotten load. Heat is dissipated in components, not lost from a node."},
 {id:"p23m1q4", q:"You just measured a motor's current with the red lead in the meter's 10 A jack. Without moving the red lead, you touch the probes across a 12 V battery to read its voltage. What happens?", options:["The meter reads 12 V, because auto-ranging detects voltage on its own","The meter's current shunt short-circuits the battery, blowing the fuse or meter","The meter reads 0 V harmlessly, because the dial was turned to volts","The meter reads the battery's internal resistance instead of its terminal voltage"], answer:1, why:"In the current jack the meter is a near-zero-ohm shunt, so placing it across a battery is a short circuit through the meter. Current is measured in series and voltage in parallel; move the lead back to the voltage jack every time you finish a current reading."},
 {id:"p23m1q5", q:"A 220 µF capacitor in a power supply is charged to 300 V. How much energy does it hold, and with a 2.2 kΩ power resistor across it, how long until it is below 1%?", options:["About 0.033 J; about 0.48 s with that resistor","About 9.9 J; about 0.48 s, one time constant","About 19.8 J; about 2.4 s, five time constants","About 9.9 J; about 2.4 s, which is five time constants"], answer:3, why:"E = ½CV² = 0.5 × 220 × 10⁻⁶ × 300² = 9.9 J. τ = RC = 2,200 × 220 × 10⁻⁶ ≈ 0.48 s, and five time constants (about 2.4 s) bring it below 1%; confirm with the meter. One τ still leaves about 37%, and ½CV forgets to square V."}
],
"p23m2":[
 {id:"p23m2q1", q:"An SMD resistor is marked 473 and a ceramic capacitor is marked 222. What are their values?", options:["473 Ω and 222 pF","4.7 kΩ and 22 nF","47 kΩ and 2.2 nF","47 kΩ and 22 nF"], answer:2, why:"Both codes are two digits followed by a power-of-ten multiplier: 47 × 10³ Ω = 47 kΩ, and 22 × 10² pF = 2,200 pF = 2.2 nF. Reading the digits as the value ignores the multiplier, and the other options misplace the exponent by one."},
 {id:"p23m2q2", q:"Two green LEDs (forward voltage 2.1 V each) must each run at 20 mA from a 12 V supply. Which arrangement is correct?", options:["Both in series with one 390 Ω resistor, which dissipates about 0.16 W","Both in parallel on one 195 Ω resistor carrying 40 mA in total","One LED with a 495 Ω resistor; the second LED needs no resistor","Both in series with a 600 Ω resistor, since 12 V ÷ 20 mA = 600 Ω"], answer:0, why:"In series the LEDs share 20 mA and drop 4.2 V together: R = (12 − 4.2) ÷ 0.02 = 390 Ω, and I²R = 0.0004 × 390 ≈ 0.16 W suits a ¼ W part. Paralleling LEDs on one resistor lets the lowest-Vf part hog the current, and ignoring Vf gives 600 Ω."},
 {id:"p23m2q3", q:"A 5 V Arduino pin must switch a 150 mA relay coil through an NPN transistor used as a saturated low-side switch, designing for a forced β of 10. What base resistor should you use?", options:["About 2.9 kΩ, using the transistor's typical hFE of 100 from the datasheet","About 333 Ω, from 5 V ÷ 15 mA of base current","About 33 Ω, from 5 V ÷ 150 mA","About 290 Ω (use 270 Ω), plus a flyback diode across the coil"], answer:3, why:"A forced β of 10 means Ib = 150 ÷ 10 = 15 mA, and Rb = (5 − 0.7) ÷ 0.015 ≈ 287 Ω, so use 270 Ω. Designing to hFE of 100 gives too little base current to saturate reliably, and 333 Ω forgets the 0.7 V base-emitter drop."},
 {id:"p23m2q4", q:"A 3.3 V circuit draws 300 mA from a 9 V supply. How do a linear regulator and a 90%-efficient buck converter compare?", options:["A linear burns about 0.17 W, so it needs no heatsink at all","A linear burns about 0.99 W, the same as the load, so either works","A linear burns about 1.7 W, while the buck wastes about 0.1 W","A linear burns about 2.7 W, which is 9 V × 0.3 A"], answer:2, why:"A linear regulator dissipates (Vin − Vout) × I = 5.7 × 0.3 ≈ 1.7 W as heat. The load uses 3.3 × 0.3 = 0.99 W, and at 90% efficiency the buck draws 1.1 W, losing about 0.1 W. 2.7 W is the total input power, not the loss."},
 {id:"p23m2q5", q:"A sensor's datasheet lists supply current typical 1.2 mA, maximum 2.5 mA (at 85 °C); absolute maximum supply 6.0 V; recommended operating supply 2.7–5.5 V. What should you design to?", options:["1.2 mA, with any supply up to 6.0 V","2.5 mA worst case, with a supply inside 2.7–5.5 V","1.2 mA, since typical is what real parts draw, at 5.5 V","2.5 mA, with a 6.0 V supply to use the part's full range"], answer:1, why:"Design to min and max, never typical, so budget 2.5 mA. The supply belongs inside the recommended operating range; the absolute maximum is the edge of damage, not a target, and running at 6.0 V leaves no margin at all."}
],
"p23m3":[
 {id:"p23m3q1", q:"An Arduino UNO's 5 V TX line must feed an ESP32 RX pin (3.3 V, not 5 V-tolerant) in one direction at 9,600 baud. Which interface is correct?", options:["Wire it directly, since the ESP32 reads anything above 2.5 V as HIGH","Add a 10 kΩ pull-up from the ESP32 RX pin to the UNO's 5 V rail","A divider with 2 kΩ on top and 1 kΩ to ground, giving about 1.67 V","A divider with 1 kΩ on top and 2 kΩ to ground, giving about 3.33 V"], answer:3, why:"5 × 2 ÷ (1 + 2) ≈ 3.33 V is a safe HIGH for the ESP32 and fine for a slow one-way signal. A direct wire puts 5 V on a 3.3 V-only pin, beyond its absolute maximum; the reversed divider gives 1.67 V, below the ESP32's roughly 2.5 V HIGH threshold."},
 {id:"p23m3q2", q:"A battery device's button uses a 4.7 kΩ pull-up to 3.3 V, and the button is held down for two hours a day. What current flows while it is pressed, and what does changing to 47 kΩ do?", options:["About 0.70 mA; 47 kΩ cuts it to 70 µA, at the cost of slower, noisier edges","None, since a pull-up conducts current only while the button is released","About 7 mA; 47 kΩ cuts it to 0.7 mA and changes nothing else about the input","About 0.70 mA; 47 kΩ cuts it to 70 µA and makes the input no worse at all"], answer:0, why:"Pressed, the resistor sits across the supply: 3.3 V ÷ 4.7 kΩ ≈ 0.70 mA, and 47 kΩ gives about 70 µA. A weaker pull-up saves power but gives slower edges and less noise immunity, which is the trade-off the value sets."},
 {id:"p23m3q3", q:"A 555 timer in monostable mode uses R = 47 kΩ and C = 22 µF. How long is the output pulse after one trigger?", options:["About 0.72 s","About 1.03 s","About 1.14 s","About 11.4 s"], answer:2, why:"The monostable pulse width is T = 1.1 × R × C = 1.1 × 47,000 × 0.000022 ≈ 1.14 s. The 0.693 factor belongs to the astable high and low times and gives 0.72 s, and R × C alone gives 1.03 s."},
 {id:"p23m3q4", q:"You need 24 independent LED outputs, but only three microcontroller pins are free. Which design works?", options:["Three 74HC165 shift registers daisy-chained, which expand outputs","Three 74HC595 shift registers daisy-chained on data, clock and latch","One 74HC74, whose two flip-flops each store twelve output bits","A 555 timer on each pin, multiplying each pin into eight outputs"], answer:1, why:"A 74HC595 shifts serial data in one bit per clock and latches eight parallel outputs, and the chips daisy-chain, so three pins drive 24 outputs. The 74HC165 does the reverse, turning parallel inputs into serial data, and a D flip-flop stores one bit, not twelve."},
 {id:"p23m3q5", q:"A logic analyzer on a new board's I2C bus shows SDA and SCL never rising to HIGH, even when the bus should be idle, and no START condition is ever decoded. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The external pull-up resistors are missing or not powered","The sensor is set to a different 7-bit address","The SPI clock mode (CPOL and CPHA) is set wrong","The baud rate does not match the sensor's setting"], answer:0, why:"I2C lines are open-drain: devices only pull them LOW, and pull-up resistors make them HIGH, so without pull-ups the bus can never idle HIGH or form a START. A wrong address would still show a START, the address and a NACK; SPI modes and baud rates do not apply."}
],
"p23m4":[
 {id:"p23m4q1", q:"An RP2040's 12-bit ADC, referenced to 3.3 V, returns a reading of 2,482. What input voltage does that represent?", options:["About 2.00 V","About 2.48 V","About 8.0 V","About 12.1 V"], answer:0, why:"Twelve bits give 4,096 steps over 3.3 V, about 0.81 mV each, so 2,482 × 3.3 ÷ 4,095 ≈ 2.00 V. Treating the count as millivolts gives 2.48 V, and using the UNO's 10-bit scale (1,023 steps, 4.88 mV each) gives impossible values above the reference."},
 {id:"p23m4q2", q:"An ESP32 LEDC channel is configured for 5 kHz PWM at 12-bit resolution. Which duty value gives a 25% duty cycle?", options:["64","25","3,072","1,024"], answer:3, why:"12-bit resolution gives 4,096 steps, and 25% of 4,096 is 1,024. 64 is 25% of an 8-bit scale such as the UNO's analogWrite, 3,072 is a 75% duty cycle, and 25 treats the duty value as a percentage."},
 {id:"p23m4q3", q:"This UNO code misbehaves: int count = 0; ISR(INT0_vect){ count++; Serial.print(count); delay(10); }, with loop() also reading count. What should change?", options:["Nothing, as long as interrupts arrive less than once every 10 ms","Declare count volatile; the ISR only updates it and returns, and loop() prints","Move the ISR inside loop() so that both can share the variable count","Keep delay(), but raise the baud rate so that Serial.print finishes in time inside it"], answer:1, why:"Shared variables must be volatile so loop() rereads them, and an ISR must be short: delay() depends on timer interrupts that are blocked inside an ISR, and Serial.print can hang there. Update the count, return, and do the slow work in loop()."},
 {id:"p23m4q4", q:"A battery-powered soil sensor must read two analog probes and send the results over Wi-Fi every 15 minutes, sleeping in between. Which board fits the job?", options:["An Arduino UNO R3, since its 5 V I/O is the most robust","A Raspberry Pi 5, since it runs Linux and has built-in Wi-Fi","An ESP32, with Wi-Fi built in, deep sleep, and both probes on ADC1 pins","An STM32 Blue Pill, since its 72 MHz clock leaves plenty of headroom"], answer:2, why:"The ESP32 is the radio chip, with Wi-Fi built in and microamp deep sleep; its ADC2 pins stop working while Wi-Fi is on, so the probes go on ADC1. A Pi has no ADC and idles at watts, and the UNO and Blue Pill have no radio."},
 {id:"p23m4q5", q:"An UNO sketch with dozens of Serial.println(\"…long status text…\") calls crashes at random, and the compiler reports 1.9 KB of the 2 KB SRAM used by globals. What is the likely cause and fix?", options:["Flash is full; move part of the program into the 1 KB EEPROM to free it","The 16 MHz clock is too slow for that much printing; fit a 20 MHz crystal","The serial baud rate is too high for the buffer; drop it to 9,600 baud","String literals fill SRAM until the stack collides; keep them in flash with F()"], answer:3, why:"The AVR is a Harvard machine, so string constants are copied into the 2 KB SRAM unless wrapped in F(). With 1.9 KB taken by globals, the stack grows down into them and the sketch crashes at random. Clock speed and baud rate do not change memory use."}
],
"p23m5":[
 {id:"p23m5q1", q:"A node is awake 2 s every 10 minutes at 150 mA. Board A sleeps at 50 µA; board B, with an AMS1117 regulator and a power LED, sleeps at 5 mA. What are their approximate average currents?", options:["A about 0.50 mA, B about 0.51 mA: sleep barely matters next to Wi-Fi","A about 0.55 mA, B about 5.5 mA: B's sleep current dominates its budget","A about 75 mA, B about 77.5 mA: the awake current sets both","A about 0.55 mA, B about 0.60 mA: B costs roughly 10% more"], answer:1, why:"A: (150 × 2 + 0.05 × 598) ÷ 600 ≈ 0.55 mA. B: (300 + 5 × 598) ÷ 600 ≈ 5.5 mA, ten times worse, so its battery lasts about a tenth as long. Averaging the awake and sleep currents without weighting by time gives the 75 mA figures."},
 {id:"p23m5q2", q:"Whenever an MG996R servo starts moving, the ESP32 resets with 'Brownout detector was triggered'. The servo is powered from the dev board's 5 V pin. What is the right fix?", options:["Disable the brownout detector in firmware so the board stops resetting","Lower the servo signal to 25 Hz so that the servo draws less current","Add a flyback diode across the servo's signal and ground wires at the board","Power the servo from its own 5 V supply sized for stall, with a common ground"], answer:3, why:"An MG996R can stall at about 2.5 A, far beyond the board's regulator and USB, so the rail dips and the chip browns out. A separate supply, a common ground and bulk capacitance cure it. Disabling the detector hides the dip and invites corruption, and the servo expects 50 Hz."},
 {id:"p23m5q3", q:"An HC-SR04 returns an echo pulse of 1,160 µs. Taking the speed of sound as 343 m/s, how far away is the object?", options:["About 20 cm","About 40 cm","About 2 cm","About 200 cm"], answer:0, why:"Sound travels 1,160 µs × 343 m/s ≈ 0.40 m, but that is the round trip, so the object is about 0.20 m away; the datasheet shortcut gives 1,160 ÷ 58 = 20 cm. Forgetting to halve the round trip gives 40 cm."},
 {id:"p23m5q4", q:"A 144-pixel WS2812B strip may be driven to full white. How much current can it draw, and what supply does it need?", options:["About 0.86 A, so the board's USB 5 V can supply it","About 2.9 A, so a 3 A USB-C port can supply it","About 8.6 A, so use a 5 V supply rated about 11 A or more","About 8.6 A, but a 5 A supply works because pixels share current"], answer:2, why:"Each pixel draws about 60 mA at full white (20 mA per color channel), so 144 × 60 mA ≈ 8.6 A at 5 V, and with 30% margin the supply should be about 11 A. Counting 20 mA per pixel gives the tempting 2.9 A."},
 {id:"p23m5q5", q:"You must drive a NEMA 17 stepper (1.8° per step, 1.5 A per phase) from a 3.3 V Raspberry Pi Pico. Which driver arrangement is right?", options:["A current-limiting driver such as a TMC2209 or A4988, with Vref set for 1.5 A","A ULN2003 Darlington array, as used with the 28BYJ-48 stepper","One logic-level MOSFET per coil, switched directly from the GPIO pins","An L298N bridge, since its 2 V drop protects the motor from excess current"], answer:0, why:"A NEMA 17 needs a bipolar driver that limits phase current, which the A4988, DRV8825 and TMC2209 do through the Vref trimmer. The ULN2003 suits the small unipolar 28BYJ-48, and the L298N's 2 V drop is wasted heat, not current control."}
],
"p23m6":[
 {id:"p23m6q1", q:"A freshly soldered through-hole joint is dull and grainy, and the solder sits as a ball on the lead without wetting the pad. What is it, and what is the fix?", options:["A solder bridge: remove the excess between the two pads with solder wick","A good joint: a ball shape shows that enough solder was applied to it","A cold joint: reheat pad and lead with flux until it wets both surfaces","An overheated joint: turn the iron down to 200 °C and solder it again"], answer:2, why:"A good joint is bright and concave, wetting both surfaces; a dull ball that has not wet the pad lacked heat on the pad or moved while cooling, so reheat both surfaces with fresh flux. 200 °C is below the melting point of SAC305 and barely above 63/37's."},
 {id:"p23m6q2", q:"You want to see about 50 mV of switching ripple riding on a 5 V supply rail. Which oscilloscope setup shows it?", options:["DC coupling at 2 V/div, so the whole 5 V rail stays on screen","AC coupling with a 10× probe at about 20 mV/div, edge-triggered","A 1× probe for its wider bandwidth, with DC coupling at 1 V/div","A logic-analyzer channel on the rail, sampling at 24 MSa/s"], answer:1, why:"AC coupling removes the 5 V offset so you can zoom to millivolts per division, and a compensated 10× probe loads the circuit less and has more bandwidth than 1×. At 2 V/div, 50 mV is a sliver of one division, and a logic analyzer shows only HIGH or LOW."},
 {id:"p23m6q3", q:"You need to judge the edge shape of a 16 MHz SPI clock. By the lesson's rule of thumb, what bandwidth should your oscilloscope have?", options:["16 MHz, matching the signal's frequency","32 MHz, twice the signal, as Nyquist requires","1.6 GHz, a hundred times the signal","At least 80 MHz, so in practice a 100 MHz scope"], answer:3, why:"Buy at least five times the highest frequency you care about: 5 × 16 MHz = 80 MHz, so a 100 MHz instrument. Edges contain harmonics well above the clock rate; at 16 or 32 MHz of bandwidth a square wave looks like a sine, hiding the ringing and slow edges you want to see."},
 {id:"p23m6q4", q:"In KiCad, a 1.5 A motor-supply trace on a 1 oz copper board was routed at the 0.25 mm default signal width. Using the lesson's design numbers, what should you change?", options:["Widen it to 1 mm or more, which carries about 2 A with a 10 °C rise","Nothing, since DRC passed and the fabricator accepts 0.25 mm","Narrow it to 0.2 mm to increase clearance to the nearby traces","Replace the trace with a row of vias of 0.3 mm drill size"], answer:0, why:"A 0.25 mm trace is sized for signals; by IPC-2221 a 1 mm trace in 1 oz copper carries about 2 A with a 10 °C rise, leaving margin at 1.5 A. DRC checks the fabricator's geometry limits, not current capacity, so a clean DRC does not prove the trace is wide enough."},
 {id:"p23m6q5", q:"On a new board, the I2C sensor drops out and the microcontroller sometimes resets, but only when a relay clicks. Following the lesson's fault-finding method, what do you check first?", options:["Rewrite the I2C library calls and add software retries around each read","Swap the sensor for a new one and see whether the dropouts persist","Scope the 3.3 V rail at the chip, triggered on the relay drive signal","Reflash the firmware after choosing a different board type in the IDE"], answer:2, why:"Power comes first: measure every rail at the chip under the failing load. A relay coil's current step and inductive kick commonly dip or spike a shared rail, which explains both the dropouts and the resets. Code, parts and IDE settings come after the rail is proven healthy."}
],
"p23m7":[
 {id:"p23m7q1", q:"A sketch toggles an LED with delay(500) inside loop(), and button presses are often missed. Which rewrite fixes it?", options:["Move the delay(500) into the button's interrupt service routine instead of loop()","Swap delay(500) for delayMicroseconds(500000) to get finer timing","Busy-wait with while (millis() < last + 500) {} before each toggle","Toggle when millis() - last >= 500, update last, and read the button every pass"], answer:3, why:"The non-blocking pattern checks elapsed time and returns at once, so the loop keeps reading the button. Every other option still blocks: a busy-wait is delay() written by hand (and its comparison breaks at rollover), and a delay inside an ISR stalls everything."},
 {id:"p23m7q2", q:"On an ESP32 running FreeRTOS, a UART byte arrives in an interrupt handler and must be processed by a parsing task. What is the correct handoff?", options:["Send it with xQueueSendFromISR and let the task block on xQueueReceive","Call the full parser directly inside the ISR so no byte is ever lost","Store it in a global and have the task spin checking it without delaying","Take the I2C mutex inside the ISR, then print the byte with Serial.print"], answer:0, why:"A queue passes data safely from an interrupt to a task: the ISR stays short, and the task sleeps until data arrives, using no CPU. Parsing in the ISR blocks other interrupts, a spinning task starves lower priorities, and blocking calls and mutexes do not belong in an ISR."},
 {id:"p23m7q3", q:"An ESP32 counts its wake-ups in an ordinary global variable, int boots = 0, incremented at startup. After every deep sleep the count is back at 1. Why, and what is the fix?", options:["The variable needs to be volatile so that the compiler keeps it","Deep sleep restarts the program from reset; keep it in RTC memory or NVS","Deep sleep corrupts SRAM, so replace it with a 15-minute delay() to keep the count","Light sleep loses RAM as well, so the counter cannot be kept at all"], answer:1, why:"Deep sleep powers down the CPU and main RAM, and the program restarts from the reset vector, so ordinary globals reinitialize. RTC memory (RTC_DATA_ATTR) or NVS flash survives. Light sleep, by contrast, keeps RAM and resumes in place, and delay() would drain the battery."},
 {id:"p23m7q4", q:"A garage node publishes temperature. Dashboards that connect later must see the latest reading at once, and the broker should announce 'offline' if the node vanishes. Which MQTT features provide this?", options:["QoS 2 on every publish, which stores the last value for new subscribers","Subscribe each dashboard to home/# so it receives the full history","A keepalive of 0, so the broker holds the connection open forever","Publish with the retain flag, and register a Last Will of 'offline'"], answer:3, why:"A retained message is delivered to every new subscriber, and the Last Will is published by the broker when the client disappears without disconnecting, noticed through the keepalive. QoS governs only delivery guarantees, and wildcards match topics but deliver no history."},
 {id:"p23m7q5", q:"A team's OTA plan fetches firmware over HTTP from a LAN server, checks it against an MD5 checksum served beside it, and leaves JTAG enabled for field debugging. What is the most serious weakness?", options:["MD5 is too slow on an ESP32, so switch to CRC32 to make updates quicker","Nothing serious: the server is on the LAN and checksums catch corruption","A LAN attacker can serve a rogue image and checksum; add TLS, signing, JTAG off","OTA needs three app partitions rather than two, so that rollback has room"], answer:2, why:"A checksum served beside the image proves only that the download matches what an attacker sent. Fetch over verified TLS, run only images whose signature Secure Boot checks, and fuse off JTAG, because an open debug port lets anyone read out or replace the firmware."}
]
});
